WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.820
We have a voicemail number.

00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.649
It's two one nine two David P..

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:07.759
Here's a very fair question about the death
rate in different states from Corona virus.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:09.180
Take a listen.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.280
Hi, David.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.210
Short term member here, I was wondering if
you could walk me and possibly the show through

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:27.400
why New York State has a relatively high death
rate for moment and stapling Florida and California

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.720
have significantly lower death rates.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.679
Are those states lying or something else going
on?

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:36.789
Sara, thanks.

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:41.340
So this is a pretty big topic and certainly
it's possible that some states are just lying.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.450
So we don't know the real deaths per capita.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.289
But let's assume the data are accurate, which
are that in New York, the death rate has been

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:53.640
about seventeen hundred per million from the
virus, whereas in Florida it's been only 500

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.680
per million.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.539
There's a couple things to consider.

00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:00.239
And the real answer is probably a combination
of all of these.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.170
First of all, the places that were hit earlier
by virtue of having more direct flights to

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.729
the source areas of infection, which are China
and certain countries in Europe like New York,

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.850
got hit and overwhelmed before we understood
we were going to have to do massive social

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:16.850
distancing.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:22.329
So the hospitals, whenever hospitals get overwhelmed
or close to it, the care suffers.

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:24.939
And you would expect a higher death rate.

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:33.659
Number two, we also now are doing a better
job of keeping the hospitals less full.

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:39.289
And so that is helping to keep the death rate
down in some of the later infection hotspots.

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:41.880
But number three, it's also a question of
timing.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:48.310
If this goes on for a long time, you would
expect because remember, deaths per capita

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:53.320
never go down because the population at least
over it, you know, the population is relatively

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.440
fixed.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.479
And so over time.

00:01:55.479 --> 00:02:00.320
Time, the death rate per capita is just going
to keep going up in places in every state,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.659
but it's going to go up faster in certain
places than others.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:05.160
So it's a perfectly reasonable question.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.530
When you get hit earlier, you're less prepared
to deal with the virus.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.330
Also, now that there are some treatments with
some level of effectiveness, dexamethasone

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:19.170
as one and there are others that are considered
to be somewhat effective to different degrees,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.040
you would expect that that would also keep
deaths down relative to the number of cases

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.090
so relatively well studied.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.500
A fair question and a good one to understand.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.070
Also important in thinking about how we deal
with what is expected to be a spike in the

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:39.300
fall as well, because states that are less
well resourced when it comes to medical resources

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.340
are going to more quickly get overwhelmed.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.920
If we don't control the spread as the flu
season coincides with possibly a spike in

